<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italic League</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1454">1454</a>–<a href="/years/1455">1455</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Italic League was an alliance concluded in 1454–1455 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and joined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, following the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a>. Its members promised to defend one another and the existing borders for twenty-five years. It helped preserve a balance of power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for some four decades, until the French invasion of 1494.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italic-league.jpg" alt="Italic League" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italy_1494_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the first half of the 15th century the larger Italian powers had been consolidating their territories. Savoy expanded towards the Ligurian coast, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> concentrated on its mainland Terraferma while Turkish expansion threatened its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stato-da-mar">Stato da Màr</a>. Milan expanded southwards and kept the bulk of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> even after the empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> broke up at his death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> had gained most of Tuscany, the Papal States had begun an expansion in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and King Alfonso V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> had extended his rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The league, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-league">Most Holy League</a>, was concluded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> on 30 August 1454, a few months after the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a>. It was solemnly proclaimed on 2 March 1455, when Pope Nicholas V, King Alfonso and other small states acceded to it; Malatestine Rimini was excluded at Alfonso's insistence. The members forbade separate alliances and treaties and committed themselves to maintaining the established boundaries.</p><p>The other Italian states acknowledged the condottiero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, who had married the only daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-maria-visconti">Filippo Maria Visconti</a>, as successor to the last of the Visconti of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and the peace allowed him to consolidate his rule there. Ending the traditional rivalry between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and Sforza's Milan was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a>'s most important foreign policy decision.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The league underpinned the balance of power later pursued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a>; its only cracks were the Pazzi conspiracy, the Barons' conspiracy and the Salt War. Its stability let the economy of the peninsula recover from the losses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>, in an expansion that lasted into the 17th century. To watch over the ban on supporting exiled dissidents, the states created the first permanent embassies among themselves, and in 1490 Ermolao Barbaro wrote De Officio Legati, apparently the first treatise on ambassadorship.</p><p>The death of Lorenzo in 1492 marked the decline of the league. It failed to prevent the French invasion of 1494 that began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>, though as the League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> it raised the army that fought Charles VIII at Fornovo after his sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. The Venetian alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and Naples in the Italian Wars of 1499–1504 ended it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-guicciardini">Francesco Guicciardini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a> later debated why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> had not united into a single monarchy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Italic League” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Politically, the Milanese claim of the house of Orléans drew France into Italy and made the duchy the cockpit of the Italian Wars, while the Peace of Lodi and the <b>Italic League</b> of 1455 offered an early model of a balance of power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
